you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  oil gas and coal developments need to  stop immediately if the world is to  reach net zero by 2050  according to the international energy  agency  six months on from the government's plan  for a green industrial revolution we  hear what progress has been made  in an exclusive interview with the  business secretary quasi quatting  and everyone's talking about pete we  hear why it's so important in tackling  climate change  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions because solutions are going to  be key to keeping  this number here below 1.5 degrees now  this shows  the level of human-induced global  warming since the industrial revolution  and as you can see it's ticking out the  whole time  global warming of more than 1.5 degrees  celsius could potentially expose the  world  to more extreme weather greater economic  losses  lower crop yields higher sea levels and  greater damage to wildlife and to  habitats  in order to limit that warming countries  need to take immediate action  radically changing the way energy is  produced used and transported  that's the view of the international  energy agency which has set out more  than  400 milestones the world's energy sector  needs to meet  if we're to stand any chance of reaching  net zero emissions  by 2050. so they say that investments in  new oil and gas projects  need to stop immediately and no more  coal-fired power plants  can be built if emissions are to be cut  to the level that many countries have  committed to  the iea recommends that by 2035 cars  with internal combustion engines  should no longer be on sale anywhere and  by 2040 the entire electricity sector  should be net zero powered entirely by  renewable sources well earlier i spoke  to the executive director of the  international energy agency  fatih birol and asked him to explain  what needs to happen  if the world is to reach that net zero  target by 2050.  number one we have to make  the most out of the existing  clean energy options such as  solar wind electric cars  energy efficiency we have to make the  most out of them  this is number one number two in  parallel to that  we need some other technologies clean  energy technologies which are under  development  but not yet part of the market today  such as hydrogen  such as advanced battery technologies we  need them  especially to decarbonize  the sector such as iron steel cement  aviation trucks we need  this new technology this is the second  one third  we have to drastically reduce the use of  fossil fuels oil gas and coal  broadly this three important categories  need to be addressed and the way doing  this  is through investments investments in  clean energy technologies is the uk  wrong in your view not to have banned  then new oil and gas exploration in the  north sea  i mean uk government is  making exemplary steps to be honest with  you  when you look at what they do at home  and then with  what they do outside and whether or not  governments in uk in u.s europe  china indonesia if they  still give licenses  for the coal or oil or gas  investments or exploration this in my  view  poses two challenges number one  whether or not they contradict  with their own domestic climate coils  is number one and number two as i said  a the we need to  reduce the oil use gas use  coal used drastically and some of those  oil production gas production coal  production  investments may vary be drunk  investments  and the governments or companies may  then lose uh money  uh as a result of uh such uh investments  so that's what the international energy  agency thinks governments  should be doing but what's the reality  well back in november  the uk government announced a 10-point  plan for a green industrial revolution  the aim is to build back better from the  coronavirus pandemic  support green jobs and accelerate  progress towards reaching  net zero well joining me now from  westminster is the business  secretary quasi quattang hello to you  first of all i want to get your reaction  to what the iea is saying they're  talking about  how action is needed now if emissions  targets are going to be reached they say  there should be no  investment in new fossil fuel supply  projects  so will you commit to a ban on new oil  and gas exploration in the north sea  we're not going to commit to it because  as you know there are 250 000 jobs  but what we are committed to is  affecting a transition and that's why  uh i negotiated to help to negotiate a  transition deal  uh and we published that transition deal  it's called the north sea transition  deal  in february and it was well widely uh  received uh in uh  well-received indeed and dr beryl was  quite right i mean  he was good enough to say that the  measures of the uk government are  exemplary that was the word he used  because other countries around the world  are looking at our 10-point plan  they see the fact that we've taken coal  off  the electricity power system um and they  think that we're we're  taking steps in the right in the right  direction does that mean that we  we shouldn't uh increase our efforts  uh no we have to increase our efforts we  have to do  this better and faster but i think we're  going in the right direction and it was  very clear from  dr beryl's words that he he shares that  view  so six months since that 10-point plan  what concrete progress can you report  so one of the things that i was very  pleased to do was only a few weeks ago  go up to t-side which was declared as a  free port  and in t site ge renewables  who make wind turbines for offshore wind  farms  they've announced 142 million pound  investment  and something like 500 jobs that will be  created  directly from that only today they've  announced the number of jobs that they  think they can  that they think they can create from it  uh we've already  uh committed to a fourth auction round  for offshore wind  we have something like 35 percent of the  global capacity global  uh worldwide capacity offshore wind is  here in the uk  uh and that we want to build on that  success so there are lots of things that  we're doing  uh that i think will uh will drive uh  the global move to net zero  quarter thank you thank you in today's  other climate news  just 20 companies are responsible for  producing more than  half of the world's single-use plastic  according to the plastic waste makers  index  the report lists exxon mobil dao and  cynopek as some of the biggest sources  of plastic food packaging and bottles  despite the environmental impact of  single-use plastic production is set to  grow by  30 percent in the next five years  supercar brand lamborghini will debut an  electric model by 2030.  the company also says it will invest 1  billion pounds to electrify  some of their existing models with aims  to reduce co2 emissions by 50 percent  by 2024. chief executive stephen  winkelmann says the change is  necessary in a radically changing world  [Applause]  and leonardo dicaprio is donating more  than 30 million  pounds to restore the galapagos islands  the actor has partnered with  conservation charity  rewild to help endangered animals and  protect the island's wildlife  they aim to reintroduce 13 locally  extinct  species including the pink iguana and  the floriana  giant tortoise arnold schwarzenegger  wants people to have a better  understanding of what cop 26  is all about the terminator star has  been speaking to the summit's president  alok sharma  about this year's climate conference in  glasgow it's part of a new monthly web  series called  arnold's stantish in which arnie talks  to environmental leaders around the  world  so how do you feel about being the  president of something that everything  you've done up until now everyone knew  what it is and now people  say i don't know really what it is well  i i think by the end of this year so  we're going to have this event in  glasgow as you know in november  and i very much hope by the end of this  year everyone does know what this is  because  uh you know this is about tackling  climate change and that is  the biggest challenge i feel that is  facing humanity  right now and you can see more of  arnie's chat with alok on our website  just head to skynews.com climate  now the government has announced plans  to restore peakland  increase tree planting and set a legal  target to stop the decline of nature and  wildlife  it's all part of plans to tackle the  climate crisis and help reach net zero  emissions by 2050.  well in a moment we'll hear more about  plans to treble tree planting in  england but first let's look in more  detail at those plans to ban  the sale of pete compost by 2024. our  science correspondent  thomas moore is here now so thomas tell  us more about pete  well pete is one of the unsung heroes of  the climate story it  stores three times as much carbon as the  same area of woodland  and that's because the plants that grow  in these boggy areas don't fully  decompose when they die  they just get buried in the waterlogged  soil now in the uk we've got 12  of our land area covered by peat and it  stores  3 billion tons of carbon and if it was  in a healthy state could  it could to store another three million  a year sucking that carbon out of the  atmosphere  the problem though that a lot of it is  being drained or is damaged  to make way for lifestyle crops and  conifer plantations  and that means that as the peat dries  out it's releasing  20 million tons a year of carbon back  into the atmosphere  so instead of being a carbon sink it's a  carbon source  and the government's recognized that the  environment secretary today  said that there will be a pete action  plan and as you say  uh pete compost will be banned uh in  garden centers from 2024 in england  and it's also funding the restoration of  35 million  uh 35 000 hectares of pete  in in the uk thomas thank you  well as well as the plans to ban pete  the government has also announced that 7  000  hectares of new woodland will be planted  every year by 2024.  and a new multi-million pound fund will  provide more incentives for landowners  and farmers  to plant and manage trees as becky  cottrell reports  [Applause]  these young trees are set to become a  vital part of this farm's ecosystem  small willow saplings are being planted  not in a wood  but in a vegetable field this is  agroforestry  we all know that trees capture carbon  but they're also  a massive tool for farmers they can  provide you  extra crops above your vegetable crops  such as apples and pears and things like  that like we do here  but they can also they provide wind  protection for my crops  these trees don't just capture emissions  they also attract songbirds  that get rid of the insects that attack  the crops meaning there's no need for  chemical pesticides  so planting here is efficient  sustainable  and it makes financial sense the  government hopes giving out agroforestry  grants  will help to meet their planting targets  about 20  of our countryside needs to be  repurposed for  climate uh uses the great news about  using trees in woodland to capture  carbon  is that they can also be landscape and  nature friendly  support wildlife support farming and be  a beautiful part of our countryside  ministers campaigners and many  landowners say farms need to be used to  reduce  emissions but growers like andy worry  food production will be driven abroad  to meet planting targets at home unless  farmers are given the support he says  they need to help us become carbon  neutral  becky cottrell sky news gloucestershire  that's all for today coming up tomorrow  the eden project in cornwall starts  drilling for hot rocks to generate  geothermal energy  thanks for watching see you then  you

